  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Dos Santos studied electrical engineering in London and opened an early restaurant business in Luanda. She subsequently accumulated stakes in major Angolan and Portuguese companies, including mobile operator Unitel, Banco BIC, Banco BPI and cable group ZON. These holdings made her one of Africa’s most visible business figures and the continent’s first woman recognised internationally as a billionaire.\n\nHer commercial rise was inseparable from her family’s political position. Investigative reporting and later legal proceedings alleged that public contracts, state-company decisions and related-party transactions helped transfer valuable assets to her network; she has denied wrongdoing. The resulting freezes and disputes dismantled much of her international portfolio and left its provenance central to any account of her wealth.",
    "html": "<p>Dos Santos studied electrical engineering in London and opened an early restaurant business in Luanda. She subsequently accumulated stakes in major Angolan and Portuguese companies, including mobile operator Unitel, Banco BIC, Banco BPI and cable group ZON. These holdings made her one of Africa’s most visible business figures and the continent’s first woman recognised internationally as a billionaire.</p>\r\n<p>Her commercial rise was inseparable from her family’s political position. Investigative reporting and later legal proceedings alleged that public contracts, state-company decisions and related-party transactions helped transfer valuable assets to her network; she has denied wrongdoing. The resulting freezes and disputes dismantled much of her international portfolio and left its provenance central to any account of her wealth.</p>\r\n",
